[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Update flow with curl, hawkBit backend (/docs/pr-preview/pr-56/platform/updates/guides/hawkbit-curl)\n\n\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The same update as in the [native guide](/docs/platform/updates/guides/native-curl), against the service in hawkBit mode. The management requests go to Lamassu Updates. The device side is a different protocol: devices poll hawkBit's DDI API, not this service and not WFX. The commands were run against a real hawkBit 1.1.0, and the responses are real, trimmed where long. Examples that say they were not run were checked against the code only.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The differences from native mode, in short:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  |                            | Native                                                                 | hawkBit                                                               |\n  | -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | Build                      | Upload artifacts and a sw-description to the pack, then build the pack | Build a **module** in the catalog first, then create the pack from it |\n  | Pack after creation        | `draft`                                                                | Already `built`                                                       |\n  | Who tells the device       | WFX job                                                                | hawkBit action, read over DDI                                         |\n  | Device reports to          | WFX                                                                    | hawkBit (DDI feedback)                                                |\n  | Next batch, with `auto` on | The service, from WFX events                                           | hawkBit, from its rollout groups                                      |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Before you begin [#before-you-begin]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * The [stack is running](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode) in hawkBit mode: this service, its Postgres, hawkBit and the Lamassu device manager.\n  * A device group in Lamassu with devices in it. This guide uses `fleet-a` with `dev-001` to `dev-004`.\n  * `curl` and `jq`.\n  * A SWU builder configured (`swugenerator_path`), to build a `swu-build` module. Without one, `POST .../build` on such a module answers `501`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  API=http://localhost:10090/updates/v1\n  HB=http://localhost:8090                                  # hawkBit\n  HB_AUTH=\"admin:${HAWKBIT_ADMIN_PASSWORD:-admin}\"          # hawkBit Management API login\n  GROUP=fleet-a\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Callout type=\"info\" title=\"Ports and gateways\">\n    `config-hawkbit.yml` in the repository serves the API on port `10090`, like the native stack, so run one backend at a time on that port. `config-hawkbit-demo.yml` serves it on `10091`, which the repository's dashboard development setup points at for the hawkBit backend. Set `API` to wherever your instance listens. Through a Lamassu gateway the API is usually `https://lamassu.example.com/api/updates/v1` and each call needs your gateway credential, for example `-H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\"`.\n  </Callout>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  1. Check the backend [#1-check-the-backend]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s $API/capabilities | jq -c '{backend, unsupported}'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"backend\":\"hawkbit\",\"unsupported\":[\"per_device_encryption\",\"workflows\",\"launch_strategy_update\",\"launch_complete\",\"launch_device_assignment\",\"push_events\",\"prebuilt_deliverable\"]}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `artifact_encryption`, `software_module_composition`, `standalone_software_modules` and `software_module_replace` appear in that list too if the build settings or hawkBit's module types are missing. See [Capabilities](/docs/platform/updates/reference/capabilities).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  2. Create a module in the catalog [#2-create-a-module-in-the-catalog]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A hawkBit distribution set can only link **built** modules, so the module comes first. `delivery_intent` says how it ships: `swu-build` builds a `.swu` from your files and a sw-description. See [hawkBit mode](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode) for the other intents (`swu-prebuilt`, `raw`).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  MID=$(curl -s -X POST $API/softwaremodules -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{\"type\":\"os\",\"name\":\"water-panel\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"delivery_intent\":\"swu-build\"}' | jq -r .id)\n  curl -s $API/softwaremodules/by-id/$MID | jq -c '{id,key,built,delivery_intent}'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"id\":\"1\",\"key\":\"os:water-panel\",\"built\":false,\"delivery_intent\":\"swu-build\"}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `type` is `os` or `application`, and `type`, `name` and `version` are required. Without a `delivery_intent` the module is `undecided`, which accepts files but cannot be built or linked until you choose one with `PUT /softwaremodules/by-id/$MID`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  3. Upload the file and the sw-description [#3-upload-the-file-and-the-sw-description]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Use the same `index.html` and `sw-description` as in the [native guide](/docs/platform/updates/guides/native-curl).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/softwaremodules/by-id/$MID/artifacts -F file=@index.html\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"id\":\"hb:1:1\",\"name\":\"water-panel\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"filename\":\"index.html\",\"checksum\":\"c400a4f1...\",\"size\":52}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/softwaremodules/by-id/$MID/descriptor -F file=@sw-description | jq -c '{id,built,artifacts: [.artifacts[].filename]}'\n  # {\"id\":\"1\",\"built\":false,\"artifacts\":[\"index.html\"]}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A `swu-build` module refuses a finished `.swu` as input, and a `swu-prebuilt` module takes exactly one `.swu`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  4. Build the module [#4-build-the-module]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/softwaremodules/by-id/$MID/build -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{}'\n  # {\"msg\":\"Software module built\"}\n\n  curl -s $API/softwaremodules/by-id/$MID | jq -c '{built, locked, files: [.artifacts[] | {filename,size}]}'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"built\":true,\"locked\":false,\"files\":[{\"filename\":\"water-panel.swu\",\"size\":1024}]}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Building replaced the module's input files with the finished `.swu`. The build body is optional. To encrypt or sign, send `user`, `encryption_key_name`, `encryption_alg_name`, `signature_key_id`, `signature_alg_name` and `signature_certificate`, as in the [signing and encryption](/docs/platform/updates/signing-and-encryption) page. A module can be built once: to change it, release a new version of the module.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  5. Create the pack from the built module [#5-create-the-pack-from-the-built-module]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/groups/$GROUP/distribution-sets -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"water-panel\\\",\\\"version\\\":\\\"1.0.0\\\",\\\"group_id\\\":\\\"fleet-a\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"rawfile\\\",\\\"packaging\\\":\\\"swu\\\",\\\"modules\\\":[{\\\"source_module_id\\\":\\\"$MID\\\"}]}\" \\\n    | jq -c '{id,name,version,status}'\n  # {\"id\":\"1\",\"name\":\"water-panel\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"status\":\"built\"}\n\n  curl -s $API/groups/$GROUP/distribution-sets | jq -r '.list[0].uri'\n  # http://localhost:8090/DEFAULT/controller/v1/{controllerId}/softwaremodules/1/artifacts/water-panel.swu\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The pack is built from the start. Its `uri` is a hawkBit download address that devices resolve themselves. In hawkBit mode `modules` takes only `source_module_id` entries, and each module must be built and compatible with the pack's packaging. Several entries make a multi-module pack, and then `GET .../swu/download` answers `400`: download each module's deliverable with `GET .../modules/:moduleKey/artifact/download`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  6. Preview the launch, then create it [#6-preview-the-launch-then-create-it]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A group's devices only exist in hawkBit once the service has registered them. The service does that whenever it lists the group's devices, which a launch, a dry run, a job listing or a compliance read all do. The canary check runs **before** that, so on the first launch of a group that has not been listed yet, a `test_device_id` fails:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST \"$API/groups/$GROUP/launch?dry_run=true\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{\"distribution_set_name\":\"water-panel\",\"workflow_type\":\"direct\",\"rollout_type\":\"fixed\",\"rollout_value\":2,\"test_device_id\":\"dev-001\"}'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"err\":\"Failed to create launch history: test device dev-001 cannot act as this group's canary: device dev-001 does not exist\"}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Run the dry run without a canary first. It registers the group's devices in hawkBit as targets and tags them `group:fleet-a`:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST \"$API/groups/$GROUP/launch?dry_run=true\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{\"distribution_set_name\":\"water-panel\",\"workflow_type\":\"direct\",\"rollout_type\":\"fixed\",\"rollout_value\":2}' | jq -c .\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"never_seen_devices\":[\"dev-001\",\"dev-002\",\"dev-003\",\"dev-004\"],\"precondition_failures\":[],\"qualifying_devices\":[\"dev-001\",\"dev-002\",\"dev-003\",\"dev-004\"],\"superseded_devices\":null}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `never_seen_devices` lists targets that have not contacted hawkBit yet. A rollout group cannot finish while its devices never poll, so check this list. `superseded_devices` lists devices that already have an update in flight: creating the launch **cancels** those actions, because hawkBit replaces a target's unfinished actions with the new one. Now create the launch with the canary:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  LAUNCH=$(curl -s -X POST $API/groups/$GROUP/launch -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{\"distribution_set_name\":\"water-panel\",\"workflow_type\":\"direct\",\"rollout_type\":\"fixed\",\"rollout_value\":2,\"test_device_id\":\"dev-001\",\"name\":\"water-panel 1.0.0 wave 1\"}' \\\n    | jq -r .launch_id)\n  echo $LAUNCH     # 1: the id of the hawkBit rollout\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `workflow_type` has no WFX meaning here: `phased` makes a hawkBit `soft` action (the device decides when to install) and anything else a `forced` one. Define a helper to follow the launch:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  status() {\n    curl -s $API/groups/$GROUP/launch | jq -c --arg id \"$LAUNCH\" \\\n      '.list[] | select(.id==$id) | {status,pending_count,active_count,completed_count,failed_count}'\n  }\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  To start the rollout later, add `scheduled_at` (RFC 3339) to the create body. hawkBit's own scheduler then starts it, and a `rollout` call before that time leaves the schedule alone. Not run here.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  7. Start the rollout [#7-start-the-rollout]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/groups/$GROUP/launch/$LAUNCH/rollout\n  # {\"message\":\"Launch history executed successfully\"}\n  status\n  # {\"status\":\"running\",\"pending_count\":3,\"active_count\":1,\"completed_count\":0,\"failed_count\":0}\n\n  curl -s -u \"$HB_AUTH\" $HB/rest/v1/rollouts | jq -c '.content[] | {id,name,status,totalTargets,type}'\n  # {\"id\":1,\"name\":\"water-panel 1.0.0 wave 1\",\"status\":\"running\",\"totalTargets\":4,\"type\":\"forced\"}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The service started a hawkBit rollout: the canary in the first group and the rest after it.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  8. Play the device (DDI) [#8-play-the-device-ddi]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A real device runs SWUpdate's `suricatta`, which does this itself. These are the requests it makes.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  First, devices must authenticate. hawkBit's default is to refuse them. Enable security tokens for the tenant once, and read the target's token:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -u \"$HB_AUTH\" -X PUT $HB/rest/v1/system/configs/authentication.targettoken.enabled \\\n    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"value\":true}'\n  TOKEN=$(curl -s -u \"$HB_AUTH\" $HB/rest/v1/targets/dev-001 | jq -r .securityToken)\n  AUTH=\"Authorization: TargetToken $TOKEN\"\n  DDI=$HB/DEFAULT/controller/v1\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  This is the local setup. In a deployment where devices reach hawkBit through the Lamassu gateway, `hawkbit.ddi_url` is the gateway path (for example `https://lamassu.example.com/api/hawkbit-ddi`) and devices authenticate as the gateway requires, typically with their client certificate. See [Connect a device](/docs/platform/updates/guides/connect-a-device).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Poll. When there is work, the answer links to a `deploymentBase`:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" $DDI/dev-001 | jq\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"config\": {\"polling\": {\"sleep\": \"00:05:00\"}},\n    \"_links\": {\n      \"deploymentBase\": {\"href\": \"http://localhost:8090/DEFAULT/controller/v1/dev-001/deploymentBase/1?c=411629670\"},\n      \"configData\": {\"href\": \"http://localhost:8090/DEFAULT/controller/v1/dev-001/configData\"}\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Read the deployment and download its artifact:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  DEP=$(curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" $DDI/dev-001 | jq -r '._links.deploymentBase.href')\n  curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" \"$DEP\" | jq -c '{action: .id, download: .deployment.download, update: .deployment.update,\n    chunks: [.deployment.chunks[] | {part,name,version, artifacts: [.artifacts[] | {filename,size}]}]}'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"action\":\"1\",\"download\":\"forced\",\"update\":\"forced\",\"chunks\":[{\"part\":\"os\",\"name\":\"water-panel\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"artifacts\":[{\"filename\":\"water-panel.swu\",\"size\":1024}]}]}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  ACTION=$(curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" \"$DEP\" | jq -r .id)\n  curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" -o water.swu -w '%{http_code} %{size_download} bytes\\n' \\\n    $DDI/dev-001/softwaremodules/1/artifacts/water-panel.swu\n  # 200 1024 bytes\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  When the service publishes the device artifact URL (the `ddi_url` path), it serves that same route itself, for modules in the device's assigned or installed set only, and with S3 presigning enabled it answers `302` to a short-lived signed URL. A device does not need to know the difference: it follows the redirect.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Report progress and the result. `closed` with `finished: success` is the end:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" -X POST $DDI/dev-001/deploymentBase/$ACTION/feedback -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{\"status\":{\"execution\":\"proceeding\",\"result\":{\"finished\":\"none\"},\"details\":[\"installing\"]}}'\n  curl -s -H \"$AUTH\" -X POST $DDI/dev-001/deploymentBase/$ACTION/feedback -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{\"status\":{\"execution\":\"closed\",\"result\":{\"finished\":\"success\"},\"details\":[\"installed\"]}}'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The service reads hawkBit when you ask, so the launch is up to date:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  status\n  # {\"status\":\"running\",\"pending_count\":3,\"active_count\":0,\"completed_count\":1,\"failed_count\":0}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  To report a failure, send `\"finished\":\"failure\"` in the `closed` feedback.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  9. Release the next batches [#9-release-the-next-batches]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  With `auto` off, hawkBit pauses after each group. Each `rollout` call releases the next one:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/groups/$GROUP/launch/$LAUNCH/rollout\n  curl -s $API/launch/$LAUNCH/jobs | jq -r '.list[] | \"\\(.clientId) \\(.status.state)\"'\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```text\n  dev-001 ACTIVATED\n  dev-002 DOWNLOADING\n  dev-003 DOWNLOADING\n  dev-004 CREATED\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Here a hawkBit action is shown as a workflow state: a scheduled action is `CREATED`, a running one `DOWNLOADING` (hawkBit does not report finer steps), a finished one (or one that finished with a warning) `ACTIVATED`, and a failed, cancelled or cancelling one `TERMINATED`. Repeat step 8 for `dev-002` and `dev-003` (the token comes from each target), then call `rollout` once more for `dev-004`. When every action is finished:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```json\n  {\"status\":\"completed\",\"pending_count\":0,\"active_count\":0,\"completed_count\":4,\"failed_count\":0}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  hawkBit decides when a batch is finished. After the last device of a batch reports, hawkBit pauses the rollout, and for a moment its group still reads `running`. The service cannot tell that pause from one an operator made, so a `rollout` call in that window answers `400` with `rollout 1 is paused; resume it first`. Call `resume` and hawkBit releases the next batch itself:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -X POST $API/groups/$GROUP/launch/$LAUNCH/resume\n  # {\"message\":\"Launch resumed\",\"status\":\"running\"}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  10. Check the result [#10-check-the-result]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s $API/devices/dev-001/distribution-set-inventory | jq -c '.list[] | {distribution_set_name,version,installed_at,artifacts: [.artifacts[].artifact_name]}'\n  # {\"distribution_set_name\":\"water-panel\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"installed_at\":\"2026-10-21T07:39:23.345+02:00\",\"artifacts\":[\"water-panel.swu\"]}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Things that behave differently [#things-that-behave-differently]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * **Automatic rollout.** With `auto: true`, hawkBit advances the groups itself once `approval_threshold` is met. The service does not run an event consumer in this mode. The thresholds are set on every rollout group, and hawkBit evaluates both percentages **per group**, not against all devices as native does. An unset or `0` threshold becomes 100, so an unset `error_threshold` never trips. The canary group always needs 100%. They apply with `auto` off too.\n  * **A tripped error threshold.** hawkBit then pauses the rollout, and the service reports the launch as `cancelled`. `resume` and `rollout` both answer `400` for it.\n  * **Pause and resume.** `pause` and `resume` map to hawkBit's rollout pause and resume, but only a pause an operator made can be resumed. hawkBit already holds the rollout between groups when `auto` is off, so `pause` then answers `400` (\"Rollout can only be paused in state running but current state is paused\") and `resume` answers `400` (\"not paused; use Execute to release the next batch\"). Use `rollout` to release the next group.\n  * **Strategy read-back.** The strategy shown for a launch is derived from the hawkBit rollout's groups, not stored. A launch created with a fixed batch of 2 for 4 devices reads back as `percentage` 50.\n  * **Static membership.** A rollout is built from the devices of the group when the launch is created. Devices added to the group later are not picked up (`launch_device_assignment` is unsupported).\n  * **Not available.** Per-device encryption, custom workflows (`GET /workflows` returns an empty list), editing a running strategy, marking a launch complete, pushed job events (progress is polled), set-level prebuilt upload, and building a pack directly. `POST .../swu` answers `501`:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```text\n  {\"err\":\"Failed to execute update pack: operation not supported by the active updates backend: hawkbit mode: a distribution set is not built directly — build each software module in the Software Modules catalog, then link the built modules into the ...\"}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Troubleshooting [#troubleshooting]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Symptom                                                                               | Cause and fix                                                                                                             |\n  | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `501` building a module: no SWU builder                                               | `swugenerator_path` is not configured. Configure it, or upload a prebuilt `.swu` with intent `swu-prebuilt`               |\n  | `501` building a module: no SymKMS or no KMS endpoint                                 | Encryption needs `symkms_endpoint` and signing needs `kms_endpoint`. Build without those fields, or configure them        |\n  | `400 ... is not built` creating a pack                                                | A module linked into a pack must be built first. Build it in the catalog                                                  |\n  | `400 ... incompatible with distribution set packaging`                                | A `raw` module cannot go into a `swu` pack, and a `swu-prebuilt` module cannot go into a `non-swu` pack                   |\n  | `400 ... frozen` changing a module                                                    | A module linked to a pack is frozen. Edit and build it in the catalog, then link it again                                 |\n  | `400 ... already built`                                                               | A module is built once. Release a new version of the module                                                               |\n  | `409 ... being built or changed by another request`                                   | Another request holds the module. Retry when it finishes                                                                  |\n  | Creating a module answers `400` mentioning that hawkBit reserves the name and version | hawkBit keeps the name and version of a module that was assigned to a device, even after it is deleted. Use a new version |\n  | The canary fails with `device ... does not exist`                                     | The group's devices are not in hawkBit yet. Run a dry run without `test_device_id` first                                  |\n  | A launch never advances                                                               | Check `never_seen_devices`: a device that never polls blocks its rollout group                                            |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Next steps [#next-steps]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * [hawkBit mode](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode) for modules, delivery intents and what lives where\n  * [Connect a device](/docs/platform/updates/guides/connect-a-device) for the device-side setup\n  * [API reference](/docs/platform/updates/reference/api)\n  * [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/updates/troubleshooting)\n</div>\n"]
